This is a known breaking change. Android deprecated `adjustResize` and .NET 10 MAUI moved to **edge-to-edge** rendering with `WindowInsets`-based keyboard handling. `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` no longer has any effect.

Here's what to do instead:

### 1. Use built-in `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` (simplest)

MAUI has a built-in auto-scroll manager (since .NET 8) that's **on by default**. If you disabled it, re-enable it. For a chat screen, make sure your input is inside a `ScrollView` or that your layout can respond to it:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" ... />
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

### 2. Handle keyboard insets manually (more control)

In your **Android platform code** (`Platforms/Android`), listen to `WindowInsets` and adjust bottom padding:

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Compatibility.Platform.Android;

public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
            Window!.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!,
            new KeyboardInsetsListener());
    }
}

class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Use the larger of IME or nav bar bottom inset
        int bottomPadding = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottomPadding);

        return insets;
    }
}
```

### 3. iOS (usually works already)

iOS uses `UIScrollView` keyboard avoidance natively. If you still have issues, set in `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
{
    Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Connect = true;
});
```

### TL;DR

| Approach | When to use |
|---|---|
| Default auto-scroll | Works for most simple layouts |
| `WindowInsets` listener | Chat screens / custom bottom bars that need precise control |

The `WindowInsets` approach (option 2) is the most reliable for a chat UI with a pinned bottom input bar.
